WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.860
Good morning, Hank, it's Monday. I love Brazil!
Did you know that Brazilian Nerdfighters petitioned

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.690
Brazilian publishers to publish LOOKING FOR
ALASKA in Portuguese and it totally worked?

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.889
God, I love Brazil. Hank, today I want to
tell you about Brazil and the United States

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:13.919
that I first heard on the amazing podcast
"Planet Money" -- there's a link to that below

00:00:13.919 --> 00:00:16.599
 -- But I also have to do some errands today
so ... walk with me.

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:21.890
So, Hank, this tee-shirt -- and yes, I'm wearing
my face on my torso -- is made from 100% American

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.089
cotton, like almost all cotton tee-shirts
in the world. Why? Because the United States

00:00:26.089 --> 00:00:28.789
massively subsidizes its cotton industry.

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:31.369
Alright I've gotta get a water filter for
my fridge. Let's drive.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:34.570
The U.S. spends more than three billion dollars
a year on its cotton subsidy, which has the

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:39.590
effect of making the price of American cotton
artificially cheap. That's a bit of an over-simplification,

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:42.050
but in my defense, I'm currently driving on
snow.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.350
Right, so these cotton subsidies don't just
make American cotton cheap in America, they

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:49.590
make American cotton cheap everywhere, which
is why the U.S. is by far the world's largest

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.900
exporter of cotton. That's great for the American
cotton farmer, but it kinda sucks for all

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.890
the non-American cotton farmers. It also sucks
for those of us who are Americans who aren't

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.840
cotton farmers, because all we get for that
3.1 billion dollars in taxes is, like, slightly

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:02.910
cheaper tee-shirts.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.750
Must resist urge to eat cheeseburgers!

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.040
So, Hank, under international trade agreements,
some agricultural subsidies are legal, but

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.340
you're not allowed to artificially lower the
price of something; and in 2002 Brazil filed

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.560
a case with the W.T.O. saying basically, when
it comes to cotton,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.950
Americans are a bunch of cheating cheaters
of cheatery.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.659
And their illegal cotton subsidies have made
it completely impossible for Brazilian farmers

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:28.380
to compete, even to sell cotton IN BRAZIL,
because American cotton is so artificially

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:29.380
cheap.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.330
In 2004 the W.T.O. sides with Brazil, says
American cotton farming subsidies are completely

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:37.149
illegal. Whereupon the United States says,
"We're rubber, you're glue; W.T.O. rulings

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:39.060
bounce off us and stick to you."

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.590
Holy snood! Adulthood is expensive.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:45.909
We completely refuse to comply, just keep
appealing, and appealing, and finally in 2008

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:51.920
we lose our last appeal, whereupon we're like,
"Ya know what? Nah, we're not gonna comply."

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.840
Which we can totally do because it turns out
there's no actual penalty for failing to follow

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.730
trade agreements. We gotta go to the grocery
store.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:01.490
So after the U.S. failed to abide by the W.T.O.'s
decision that U.S. cotton subsidies were illegal,

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.320
Brazil's only real recourse was retaliation.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.000
But if you're a small economy in a fight with
a huge economy, your retaliation options are

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.130
a little limited. Like, if Liberia doesn't
like American cotton subsidies, they can say,

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.550
"You know what? We're gonna tax all American
imports to Liberia."

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.730
And then America will be like, "... Okay."

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.390
And that's probably how this fight with Brazil
would have ended ten years ago, but what happened

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:25.891
in the meantime is that Brazil became a BIG
economy. Nowhere near the size of the U.S.,

00:02:25.891 --> 00:02:26.970
certainly, but big.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:31.190
Which is why, when Brazil threatened to tax
American imports like pharmaceuticals and

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.510
cosmetics, those companies called their friends
in the American government and said, "Ya gotta

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.670
do something about this cotton business!"

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.520
And they did come to an agreement! In April
of 2010 the United States announced that it

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:48.620
would pay Brazilian cotton farmers 147 million
dollars a year in order to continue subsidizing

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:53.260
our own cotton farmers illegally, to the tune
of three billion dollars a year.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.811
And that, Hank, is how the United States came
to subsidize the Brazilian cotton industry.

00:02:56.811 --> 00:03:00.790
If we were gonna be fair, we should subsidize
every cotton industry in the developing world,

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:05.340
but Brazil was the only country that was smart
enough to sue us and big enough to scare us.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.980
Like I said earlier, I love Brazil. But I
don't want to send their cotton farmers 147

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.260
million dollars each year.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.980
Hank, I'll see you on Wednesday.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.310
By the way, Nerdfighters, the American taxpayer
has already paid for a significant portion

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:20.400
of this tee-shirt. If you'd like to pay for
the rest of it, you can do so at dftba.com/pizza

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.150
It's in stock, ships immediately, and thanks
to bad economic policy-making, it's only $18.

